    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list><item>Invasive ductal carcinoma of the upper outer quadrant of the right breast, stage IA (pT1c pN0(sn) M0), grade 2, estrogen and progesterone receptor positive, HER2 negative, recurrence score 14; treated by right total mastectomy with sentinel node biopsy and on adjuvant tamoxifen; synthetic modern counterpart of the documented 1978 breast cancer and mastectomy: active, confirmed [S]</item><item>Estrogen receptor positive status of the breast cancer (ER 90 percent, PR 60 percent): active, confirmed [S]</item><item>Absence of the right breast after mastectomy with flat closure; she chose not to have reconstruction and not to wear an external prosthesis: active, confirmed [S]</item><item>Long-term use of tamoxifen as adjuvant endocrine therapy: active, confirmed [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Medical oncology consultation to confirm the adjuvant endocrine therapy plan, advise on hot flashes, set the surveillance plan, and advise on genetic assessment. Guideline: NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Breast Cancer, Version 5.2025 (2025); Adjuvant Endocrine Therapy for Women With Hormone Receptor-Positive Breast Cancer: ASCO Clinical Practice Guideline Focused Update (2019) [S]</item><item>Tamoxifen 20 mg daily for at least five years, with questions at every visit about hot flashes, abnormal vaginal bleeding, and leg swelling or breathlessness, and a yearly gynecologic assessment. Guideline: Adjuvant Endocrine Therapy for Women With Hormone Receptor-Positive Breast Cancer: ASCO Clinical Practice Guideline Focused Update (2019); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, breast cancer monograph (2026) [S]</item><item>Cancer-related history and examination every 3 to 6 months for the first 3 years and a yearly diagnostic mammogram of the left breast; no routine blood tests, tumor markers, or body imaging while she has no symptoms. Guideline: American Cancer Society/American Society of Clinical Oncology Breast Cancer Survivorship Care Guideline. CA Cancer J Clin 2016;66(1):43-73 (2016); Breast Cancer Follow-Up and Management After Primary Treatment: ASCO Clinical Practice Guideline Update. J Clin Oncol 2013;31(7):961-965 (2013); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, breast cancer monograph (2026) [S]</item><item>Germline genetic counseling and multigene panel testing for breast cancer diagnosed at 44. Guideline: NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Breast Cancer, Version 5.2025 (2025); Germline Testing in Patients With Breast Cancer: ASCO-Society of Surgical Oncology Guideline. J Clin Oncol 2024 (2024) [S]</item><item>Lymphedema risk counseling and arm checks at every visit; bone health by calcium and vitamin D from diet and weight-bearing exercise; distress, depression, and anxiety screening at intervals. Guideline: American Cancer Society/American Society of Clinical Oncology Breast Cancer Survivorship Care Guideline. CA Cancer J Clin 2016;66(1):43-73 (2016); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, breast cancer monograph (2026) [S]</item><item>Written survivorship care plan, at least 150 minutes of activity a week, alcohol at or below one drink a day, and her decisions about reconstruction and a prosthesis recorded and respected. Guideline: American Cancer Society/American Society of Clinical Oncology Breast Cancer Survivorship Care Guideline. CA Cancer J Clin 2016;66(1):43-73 (2016); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, breast cancer monograph (2026) [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
